{"name":"Mayon Mist","hex":"#e1e6f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mayon","blocks":[{"text":"Mayon, also known as Mount Mayon and Mayon Volcano, is an active stratovolcano in the province of Albay in Bicol, Philippines. A popular tourist destination, it is renowned for its \"perfect cone\" owing to its symmetric conical shape, and is regarded as sacred in Philippine mythology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Mount_Mayon_Cagsawa_field_view_close-up_%28Busay%2C_Daraga%2C_Albay%3B_04-21-2023%29.jpg/330px-Mount_Mayon_Cagsawa_field_view_close-up_%28Busay%2C_Daraga%2C_Albay%3B_04-21-2023%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayon","wikidata":"Q1484"},{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist","wikidata":"Q192196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Oldcagsawapi2.jpg/500px-Oldcagsawapi2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Oldcagsawapi2.jpg/500px-Oldcagsawapi2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An old photograph of the Cagsawa ruins in February 1, 1814 with the façade still standing. The church was largely destroyed during the 1814 eruption of Mayon. Only the bell tower exists today, the façade having been demolished in the 1960's or 1970's due to structural integrity concerns.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOldcagsawapi2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Mayon_1928_1.jpg/500px-Mayon_1928_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Mayon_1928_1.jpg/1280px-Mayon_1928_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Mayon_1928_1.jpg/500px-Mayon_1928_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mount Mayon erupting on July 21, 1928","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMayon_1928_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Pyroclastic_flows_at_Mayon_Volcano.jpg/500px-Pyroclastic_flows_at_Mayon_Volcano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Pyroclastic_flows_at_Mayon_Volcano.jpg/500px-Pyroclastic_flows_at_Mayon_Volcano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The eruption on September 23, 1984","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APyroclastic_flows_at_Mayon_Volcano.jpg"},{"srcset":"htt